      abstractEn: "The working alliance is among the most consistently studied correlates of psychotherapy outcome, and researchers of mental health chatbots increasingly report that users form an alliance with their software. This theoretical contribution examines whether, and in what sense, such a claim is coherent. Starting from Bordin’s pantheoretical formulation of the alliance as agreement on goals, agreement on tasks and an affective bond, we argue that the first two components can be partly realized in human–software interaction, because they concern structure, transparency and collaboration. The bond component is more problematic. Self-report studies with conversational agents such as Woebot and Wysa suggest that users describe feelings of being understood and supported, yet these reports are also compatible with the ELIZA effect, the human tendency to attribute understanding to systems that merely simulate responsiveness. Drawing on Buber’s distinction between I–Thou and I–It relations and on Frankl’s view of therapy as personal encounter, we distinguish an experienced bond from a reciprocal bond. We propose that a “digital working alliance” should be conceptualized as a user-side construct, measured with validated instruments and interpreted cautiously. Implications for research, clinical practice and transparency are discussed.",
