      abstractEn: "Large language models can conduct fluent, open-ended conversations, and they are increasingly used for reflection on personal questions, including questions of meaning. This theoretical contribution asks whether such systems can support the meaning-finding that logotherapy pursues through the Socratic dialogue. We first outline the logotherapeutic understanding of meaning as something to be discovered rather than invented or given, grounded in self-transcendence, responsibility and conscience, and describe the Socratic dialogue as a method of guided questioning that helps persons perceive the meaning potentials of their concrete situation. We then characterize large language models as probabilistic text generators whose outputs can be helpful but are prone to fabrication and to agreeing with the user. On this basis, we identify possible contributions, such as prompting reflective questions, structuring exercises and supporting therapists, as well as central risks, in particular the supply of ready-made meaning, validation without challenge and the bypassing of personal conscience. We argue that AI can at most serve as a maieutic aid that returns questions to the person, never as an authority on what is meaningful. Design principles and research questions for meaning-oriented applications are proposed.",
